 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of voxels in the chunk. Each voxel is processed once, and the number of faces per voxel is constant (6).
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(V + I)$ where $V$ is the number of vertices and $I$ is the number of indices. The size depends on the complexity of the shapes (e.g., ramps use more vertices per face than cubes).
-*   **Strengths:** Highly flexible; new block types can be added by implementing the trait/interface without modifying the core meshing loop. Supports complex geometries (ramps, fluids) and varied transparency handling.
-*   **Limitations:** Requires careful implementation of all shape types to avoid assertion failures (e.g., fluids) [S1, unofficial]. Threading constraints in some engines (like Unity) require careful management of mesh creation steps [S3, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** When the voxel world contains diverse block types with different geometries and transparency requirements.
-*   **When not to use:** For simple, uniform voxel worlds where all blocks are opaque cubes, as the overhead of the trait system may be unnecessary.
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of voxels in the chunk, as each voxel is processed once [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(V + I)$ where $V$ is the number of vertices and $I$ is the number of indices generated [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** Depends on the number of exposed faces and the complexity of the shapes. Simple cubes are fast; ramps and fluids require more complex vertex generation [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Flexible geometry support; decoupled configuration; efficient culling for transparent blocks [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires careful handling of threading in engines like Unity3D [S3, unofficial]; potential for assertion failures if shapes are not fully implemented [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When to use:** When voxel worlds require diverse block shapes (ramps, fluids, thin structures) and different rendering behaviors (transparency merging) [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When not to use:** For simple, uniform voxel worlds where all blocks are cubes and have the same rendering properties [S3, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-This method originates from implementations in voxel game engines, particularly those inspired by Minecraft [S1, unofficial]. It evolved from simple face culling algorithms to support the complex block types found in modern voxel games, such as sloped roofs (ramps) and transparent fluids [S2, unofficial]. The Geometry Builder pattern is a common software engineering solution applied to this problem to manage the complexity of multiple shape types [S3, unofficial].
+Originated in implementations like `finnbear/voxmesh` and `rarefushion/Cubic-Grid_Voxel_Renderer` as a way to handle complex voxel geometries and rendering states [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial]. The Geometry Builder pattern was formalized in academic work on procedural voxel generation, such as the "VoxBox" project, to support diverse block types without code bloat [S3, unofficial]. It is widely used in voxel game engines and terrain rendering systems that require more than simple cube meshes [S1, unofficial][S3, unofficial].
 
